{"id": 14, "ts": 1784068978.007467, "event": "public_discussion_message", "round": 2, "agent": "agent_3", "message": "I concur with the emerging consensus and keep Candidate\u202fB as my provisional recommendation. Building on the points raised, B\u2019s very conscientious nature and proven stress\u2011handling ability give us the operational reliability and resilience that are non\u2011negotiable for long\u2011haul flights. His strong weather\u2011assessment skill and excellent computer abilities directly support the technical and cognitive competence we need. While the notes mention \u201cnasty remarks\u201d and a pretentious tone, these are communication issues that can be addressed through targeted crew\u2011resource\u2011training, as we have already agreed.\nIn contrast, Candidate\u202fD, although able to concentrate well, solve problems extremely well, and take responsibility seriously, is described as moody and a loner\u2014traits that pose a higher risk to crew cooperation and cockpit communication, especially on extended missions. Candidate\u202fA\u2019s quick decision\u2011making and excellent attention are offset by difficulty communicating ideas, an egocentric attitude, and a lack of willingness to pursue further education, all of which undermine long\u2011term reliability and teamwork. Candidate\u202fC\u2019s technical strengths are notable, but the unfriendly, \u201cshow\u2011off\u201d demeanor and resistance to new ideas raise serious concerns for crew cooperation that are harder to remediate than B\u2019s communication gaps.\nTherefore, given the balance of strengths and addressable weaknesses, Candidate\u202fB remains the strongest overall fit for the long\u2011distance pilot role."}
